1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,520
In this video we'll show you the basics of creating users in Burp Suite Enterprise Edition

2
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,080
and show you how to set up role-based access control.

3
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:20,960
If you select team from the top navigation menu you can see a list of all the users,

4
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:27,120
groups and roles that have already been configured. A user can be a person who needs to log into the

5
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:32,960
web interface or you can create API users that other machines or applications can use to interact

6
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:38,320
with Burp Suite Enterprise Edition. As you can see here I've got one for my Jenkins integration for

7
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:45,920
example. We'll look at API users and integrating with CI-CD in a separate video. A role is just a

8
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,320
collection of actions that you're allowed to perform. There are several built-in roles that

9
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:55,200
you start out with so if we look at the scan initiator role for example this has access to

10
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,880
view scans, sites, issue details and edit the schedule of a scan and so on.

11
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:09,280
A group is essentially just a mapping of users to roles. So as you can see here there's a built-in

12
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:15,280
group that corresponds to each of the roles we just saw. Each of these groups has that role assigned to

13
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:21,360
it as well as a few users. As user management in Burp Suite Enterprise Edition is based on the role-based

14
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:27,600
access control model you don't assign permissions directly to individual users. Instead you create

15
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:33,200
roles that represent different types of user and then assign those to a group. Users then inherit all

16
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,600
of the permissions from whichever groups they belong to. This is a lot simpler than it sounds once you see

17
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:46,320
it in practice so let's walk through an example together. First of all I'm going to create a new role

18
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:50,400
and call it scan reviewer.

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,920
Now I just need to select all of the things that I want people with this role to be able to do.

20
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:04,400
As you can see these permissions are grouped based on the objects that the actions relate to like sites

21
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:10,160
or scans for example. You might not be able to see this in the video very well but if you look closely

22
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:14,800
you can see that some of these permissions are greyed out. This is because they're dependent on

23
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:20,320
another permission being granted first. So for example most of the scan related permissions are

24
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:25,040
greyed out here because obviously it doesn't make much sense to be able to delete scans unless you can

25
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:30,720
view them in the first place. So once we select the view scans role some of these other scan related

26
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:36,240
permissions will become available. You might find it easier to select all of the permissions in a

27
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:41,280
folder and then just deselect the ones you don't want. I'll quickly just set the rest of the permissions

28
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:47,680
that I want for this role. When you're done just click save and you can see that the new role now appears

29
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:52,640
alongside the built-in ones. So next I'll create a corresponding group

30
00:02:52,640 --> 00:03:01,040
and we'll call it scan reviewers US. I'll show you why in a second.

31
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:09,520
Now we can assign the role that we just created to this group. Note that you can actually assign

32
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,960
multiple different roles to a group but we don't need to do that for this example.

33
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,480
And now I'll just assign some of the existing users to this group.

34
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:27,840
Finally you can apply site restrictions for a group. So by default the members of a group will

35
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:32,320
be able to perform all of their permitted actions on all of the sites that you have configured.

36
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:37,600
So you can choose to limit them so that they're only allowed to interact with specific sites.

37
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:43,200
Just like with the permissions you can select these individually or just select the entire folder

38
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:50,560
and exclude specific sites if that's easier. For this example I'll let members of this group work

39
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:56,400
with all US-based sites only. And now we're done so I can go ahead and save the group.

40
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:02,960
So we've already added some existing users to this group but let's create a new one as well.

41
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:08,960
As you've probably already guessed you can do this by going to the users tab and then just clicking

42
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:15,360
new user. Or alternatively you can hover over the team menu here and just select add a new user.

43
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,720
You'll then be asked to enter some basic user details.

44
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:31,360
If this is just a normal user that needs to log into the Burpsuite Enterprise Edition web interface

45
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:37,360
just leave the login type set to password. If you were creating an API user you'd select API key here

46
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:43,120
obviously but we'll save that for another time. Then just assign them to any relevant groups.

47
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:50,400
When you go to save you'll be prompted to copy a password generation link and send this to the user

48
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:55,760
so that they can set up their initial password. If your admin user happens to have also configured

49
00:04:55,760 --> 00:05:00,880
a connection to an SMTP server already then the user will also receive an email containing this link.

50
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:08,160
Finally I just want to point out that Burpsuite Enterprise Edition also supports single sign-on

51
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:13,360
using LDAP or SAML but we'll cover this in more detail in a separate video.

52
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:20,160
So that's how to get started with user management and role-based access control in Burpsuite Enterprise Edition.

53
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:22,320
So that's how to get started with user management.

